{:edb/db-before-t 2690 :edb/db-after-t 2691 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536003 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:42:14.689Z" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161718 1100 "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2x_3+x_3^2$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/d041c39fe285ee5fdbd8022b70a8043a4ff07cbeb1dae8c49905248df1b86d01.png)\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_3^2-2x_1^2$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161718 1100 "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n1. $P(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2x_3+x_3^2$\n2. $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$, where $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_3^2-2x_1^2$" 13194139536003 false] [17592186161718 1145 "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\n). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3$.\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\"" 13194139536003 false] [17592186161718 1145 "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms ($x_i x_j$ where $i \\neq j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(\\mathbf{x})$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2x_2x_3.$\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- $R(\\mathbf{x})$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(\\mathbf{x})$ and $R(\\mathbf{x})$ only.\"" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161719 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$4$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 false] [17592186161719 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-18x_1x_2+5x_2^2$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/cc77cb76fcab6bd72211e7e66d5fee5ba87c0ba1465571aa88baf26beb196a22.png)\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161719 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (9x_{1}^{2} - 18x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9(x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9([x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}] - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9((x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - x_{2}^{2}) + 5x_{2}^{2} &= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 9x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 9(x_{1} - x_{2})^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 - x 2; y_{2} = x_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2$." 13194139536003 false] [17592186161719 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and factor out the coefficient of $x_1^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9([{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}]-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})+5{x}_{2}^{2} & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =9({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/e1048614ed3dda333597bf32339f23a1585308e630233e3d11751c48c597ed52.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $4 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_1-x_2 .$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161720 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n${y_{1} = [math]\\phantom{x_1+x_2-2x_3}[/math]; y_{2} = [math]\\phantom{x_2-3x_3}[/math]; y_{3} = x_{3}$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 false] [17592186161720 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-4x_3^2+2x_1x_2-4x_1x_3+2x_2x_3$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/bf0297b0d758d02198d01565050bc5f3b02b0964336d3b84fdca331fabed6051.png)\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161720 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - 4x_{1}x_{3}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3})) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(x_{2} - 2x_{3}) + (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2}) + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 4x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} - 4x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2$, factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2$, and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - x_{2}^{2} + 6x_{2}x_{3} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - ([x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{2}x_{3} + 9x_{3}^{2}] - 9x_{3}^{2}) - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + 9x_{3}^{2} - 8x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + x_{2} - 2x_{3})^{2} - (x_{2} - 3x_{3})^{2} + x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x 1 + x 2 - 2 x 3; y_{2} = x 2 - 3 x 3; y_{3} = x_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3$." 13194139536003 false] [17592186161720 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}))+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2})+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group $x_2^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_2,$ factor out the coefficient of $x_2^2,$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+9{x}_{3}^{2}]-9{x}_{3}^{2})-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+9{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-3{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073449/assets/84f88cbb41e573d7ed0793919e0ad25477a46637d09144ca9f462e12f0bc6e90.png)\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x_1+x_2-2x_3 \\quad$ and $\\quad x_2-3x_3.$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186161926 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379019 13194139536003 true] [17592186161926 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379033 13194139536003 true] [17592186161926 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379040 13194139536003 true] [17592186161926 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379047 13194139536003 true] [17592186161926 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379054 13194139536003 true] [17592186161927 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379061 13194139536003 true] [17592186161927 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379068 13194139536003 true] [17592186161927 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379075 13194139536003 true] [17592186161927 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379082 13194139536003 true] [17592186161928 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379026 13194139536003 true] [17592186161928 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379089 13194139536003 true] [17592186161928 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379096 13194139536003 true] [17592186161928 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379103 13194139536003 true] [17592186161928 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379110 13194139536003 true] [17592186162546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536003 true] [17592186162546 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x) = 3x_{1}^{2} + 29x_{2}^{2} + 18x_{1}x_{2}$ to the canonical form given below.\n\n$3y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2}, {y_{1} = x_{1} + 3x_{2}; y_{2} = x_{2}$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 false] [17592186162546 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 true] [17592186162546 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}]-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-27{x}_{2}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}.$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379020 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1089 "q-140982" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1100 "Which of the following quadratic forms are given in their canonical forms?\n\n- $P(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$\n- $Q(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}$\n- $R(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax,$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1113 #uuid "36a5116d-e670-58dd-8a1a-da9cc3b98bc9" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379019 1145 "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $Q(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since \n\n$$\nQ(x)=({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2}={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\n So,  it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P(x)$ only.\"" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379021 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379022 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379023 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379024 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379025 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1041 "selection" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379021 13194139536003 true] [17592186379020 1110 #uuid "d4985610-d461-59e5-89dd-ea940d6b0a2b" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379021 1034 "$P(x)$ only" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379021 1111 #uuid "be9417a3-026f-5fa7-bb97-ab92fc702019" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379022 1034 "$P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ only" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379022 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379022 1111 #uuid "a92d617a-4b4e-5add-b0b5-b948f38ab4ab" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379023 1034 "$Q(x)$ only" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379023 1111 #uuid "6565f6f3-11fb-5380-b898-1fb6f86a80f1" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379024 1034 "$Q(x)$ and $R(x)$ only" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379024 1111 #uuid "3f183202-7067-50a8-b0e4-55e7cd79b36f" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379025 1034 "$R(x)$ only" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379025 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379025 1111 #uuid "6b2d48f7-288b-5a3a-b822-317c59a9d1ad" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379027 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1089 "q-140986" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1100 "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what is missing from the blank spaces?" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1113 #uuid "c82f5129-4cd7-5326-9dc3-84b2ccee4d9c" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379026 1145 "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+40{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}))-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})+({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2})-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-6{x}_{3})^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+12{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-36{x}_{3}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+40{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group ${x}_{2}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{2},$ factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{2}^{2},$ and complete the square inside the second parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-([{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{3}^{2}]-4{x}_{3}^{2})+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3})^{2}-({x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})^{2}+8{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{3}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379028 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379029 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379030 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379031 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379032 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1041 "selection" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379030 13194139536003 true] [17592186379027 1110 #uuid "15d4c976-bc0f-5630-9d2b-0b1c3a1fa5a5" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379028 1034 "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379028 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379028 1111 #uuid "0943c76c-697f-56af-ab7d-4420b2d56bd3" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379029 1034 "${x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379029 1111 #uuid "f402b1bc-56fd-53e5-9bd5-d14f3dd16c1a" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379030 1034 "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379030 1111 #uuid "f2a159c1-f105-5c10-8585-d20a73aa7d4d" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379031 1034 "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536003 true] [17592186379031 1111 #uuid "86c628e7-32d8-56e1-be95-f3f3ec9538fd" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379032 1034 "${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}$" 13194139536003 true] [17592186379032 1103 #edb/ref 175